WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.080 --> 00:00:04.480
Film Courage: Another book of your's is Sell
Your Story To Hollywood, The Writer’s Pocket

00:00:04.480 --> 00:00:06.859
Guide - The Business of Show Business.

00:00:06.859 --> 00:00:10.240
I know there’s lots of great nuggets in
there.

00:00:10.240 --> 00:00:15.550
Something I saw from the Amazon page “There
are four things in order that I’m about

00:00:15.550 --> 00:00:22.642
to read that will guarantee success (and this
is in the book’s forward or something) Perseverance,

00:00:22.642 --> 00:00:27.450
Determination or Stamina, Connection, Be Fun
To Work With and lastly…Talent?

00:00:27.450 --> 00:00:29.270
Really, that’s the order?

00:00:29.270 --> 00:00:33.500
Dr. Ken Atchity, author/producer: Yes, that’s
definitely the order.

00:00:33.500 --> 00:00:39.160
Actually in my mind I’ve added another one
before Talent which is Luck.

00:00:39.160 --> 00:00:40.930
Luck is definitely important.

00:00:40.930 --> 00:00:46.610
But you can think about it, some movies get
made just because of perseverance.

00:00:46.610 --> 00:00:52.370
Somebody keeps persevering and they can get
the movie made and somebody can make a movie

00:00:52.370 --> 00:00:55.250
because they have connections, you know?

00:00:55.250 --> 00:00:57.860
Sophia Coppola, she got to make a movie.

00:00:57.860 --> 00:01:03.260
She’s got incredible connections and many
other people in Hollywood.

00:01:03.260 --> 00:01:06.110
Being fun to work with, that happens all the
time.

00:01:06.110 --> 00:01:07.620
Movies get made because of that.

00:01:07.620 --> 00:01:11.280
I’ve never really seen a movie get made
just because of talent.

00:01:11.280 --> 00:01:17.800
So all of these things, the three that I started
with like perseverance, etc. being fun to

00:01:17.800 --> 00:01:22.660
work with, these are sufficient causes of
movies.

00:01:22.660 --> 00:01:28.320
In other words all you need is one of those
and you’re able to make a movie but talent

00:01:28.320 --> 00:01:29.750
is not sufficient.

00:01:29.750 --> 00:01:34.740
You have to have talent plus one of those
other things to make a movie and there is

00:01:34.740 --> 00:01:38.810
plenty of talent around.

00:01:38.810 --> 00:01:42.780
So that’s the bad news, talent is not enough
to make a movie.

00:01:42.780 --> 00:01:47.990
But the good news is that if you have talent,
it’s what everybody is looking for in Hollywood,

00:01:47.990 --> 00:01:50.970
looking for somebody who is truly talented
because they don’t want movies that are

00:01:50.970 --> 00:01:56.840
just made with perseverance or being fun to
work with or having connections, they want

00:01:56.840 --> 00:01:58.500
truly good movies.

00:01:58.500 --> 00:02:02.740
So if you’ve got talent that’s great but
you need to get those other things, you need

00:02:02.740 --> 00:02:03.740
to persevere.

00:02:03.740 --> 00:02:08.630
It’s not a career for the faint-hearted.

00:02:08.630 --> 00:02:11.790
If you say you’re going to give yourself
5 years and then I’m going to go back to

00:02:11.790 --> 00:02:17.420
my horse ranch in Utah, you’ll be back there
before you know it because life loves little

00:02:17.420 --> 00:02:23.170
deals like that and it always gets you back
to the horse ranch.

00:02:23.170 --> 00:02:27.230
If you say that there will be no limit on
my career, I’m going to continue no matter

00:02:27.230 --> 00:02:33.070
what then you’ve got a chance to do it and
maybe you’ll get lucky along the way but

00:02:33.070 --> 00:02:39.560
I always say build the tracks for success,
don’t build them based on luck.

00:02:39.560 --> 00:02:45.300
So luck is an important part of it because
sometimes you can have everything ready to

00:02:45.300 --> 00:02:49.380
go and somebody comes out with a similar movie
and you can’t do it.

00:02:49.380 --> 00:02:55.020
Film Courage: Going back to the school of
thought on two types of people: unproductive,

00:02:55.020 --> 00:03:02.349
negative versus positive, what about unharnessed
talent versus harnessed talent?

00:03:02.349 --> 00:03:04.380
And I’m making my own little genre here.

00:03:04.380 --> 00:03:10.489
But there is so much talent here but there
is a lot of people that don’t harness it

00:03:10.489 --> 00:03:12.680
and they spin their wheels talking about these
ideas.

00:03:12.680 --> 00:03:14.760
How does someone go into the harnessed category?

00:03:14.760 --> 00:03:20.090
Dr. Ken: Going into the harnessed category
just means discipline to actually sit down

00:03:20.090 --> 00:03:24.730
and figure out time and use the time and just
determine to do it.

00:03:24.730 --> 00:03:28.520
You have to be determined, you have to persevere.

00:03:28.520 --> 00:03:33.670
There are a lot of people who have what I
call artistic syndrome who would like to be

00:03:33.670 --> 00:03:40.190
creative, who say they are creative, who show
some signs of creativity but creativity is

00:03:40.190 --> 00:03:48.180
actually having finished a script, having
finished a novel and continuing to write another

00:03:48.180 --> 00:03:52.150
one while you’re waiting on the reaction
from the first one.

00:03:52.150 --> 00:03:54.450
Creativity is “I can’t stop writing this.

00:03:54.450 --> 00:03:58.370
I keep getting stories and I can’t wait
to get to the next one.”

00:03:58.370 --> 00:04:02.340
So you don’t really wait at all when you
send it out.

00:04:02.340 --> 00:04:05.430
Now you can work on the next one, you keep
writing.

00:04:05.430 --> 00:04:09.270
That’s someone who is harnessing their talent.

00:04:09.270 --> 00:04:14.650
Unharnessed means when you boobytrapped your
talent by either not sitting down for fear

00:04:14.650 --> 00:04:24.580
of failure or whatever your reasons are (procrastination)…Tony
Robbins always says the best way to deal with

00:04:24.580 --> 00:04:29.289
procrastination is just to postpone it.

00:04:29.289 --> 00:04:32.490
You can do that now but right now get to work.

00:04:32.490 --> 00:04:36.599
So harnessed talent is all a matter of discipline
and determination.

00:04:36.599 --> 00:04:42.111
It starts with determination, turns it into
discipline, discipline turns into work, work

00:04:42.111 --> 00:04:46.330
is based on time allotted to it.

00:04:46.330 --> 00:04:51.889
As the ancient philosopher Isiad said if you
put a little upon a little, soon it will become

00:04:51.889 --> 00:04:52.889
a lot.

00:04:52.889 --> 00:04:59.740
You do 5 pages a day, that’s 60 pages after
12 days.

00:04:59.740 --> 00:05:01.530
So it’s just inevitable.

00:05:01.530 --> 00:05:07.629
It’s the law of accumulating capital and
if people aren’t doing that then they are

00:05:07.629 --> 00:05:09.789
completely unharnessed.

00:05:09.789 --> 00:05:13.669
And the only person who can harness a writer
is himself.

00:05:13.669 --> 00:05:22.119
So nobody can harness it for you, you can
get a manager to help you with that but at

00:05:22.119 --> 00:05:27.569
the end of the day there are more ways to
evade discipline than there are to impose

00:05:27.569 --> 00:05:31.870
it (that’s the genius of humanity right)?

00:05:31.870 --> 00:05:36.720
Film Courage: Lastly you said something about
being called to the horse ranch (giving yourself

00:05:36.720 --> 00:05:37.949
5 years).

00:05:37.949 --> 00:05:42.129
Someone just commented on our channel the
other day (and they had a valid point) “Give

00:05:42.129 --> 00:05:43.369
yourself three years.”

00:05:43.369 --> 00:05:45.379
And they were referring to acting.

00:05:45.379 --> 00:06:02.210
I agreed with a lot of their statements but

00:06:02.210 --> 00:06:29.009
they were saying “Give yourself three years
max to come to LA or wherever (you can insert

00:06:29.009 --> 00:06:30.009
writing)”

00:06:30.009 --> 00:06:34.050
Dr. Ken: That’s like give yourself a year
to go to Europe to get your wild oats out

00:06:34.050 --> 00:06:38.860
of your system and settle down and get engaged
or something.

00:06:38.860 --> 00:06:43.949
If that’s the way you’re looking at it
then it will just be a three year lark.

00:06:43.949 --> 00:06:46.830
It’s not a career commitment.

00:06:46.830 --> 00:06:52.030
There’s a great moment in a movie called
BURLESQUE (I don’t know if you saw that

00:06:52.030 --> 00:06:57.960
movie but Christina Aguilera is in it) but
one of the opening scenes in their little

00:06:57.960 --> 00:07:03.270
town…she goes to the bus station and she
stands at the ticket window and she says “I

00:07:03.270 --> 00:07:04.809
want a ticket to LA.”

00:07:04.809 --> 00:07:07.860
And he looks at her and says “One way or
round trip?”

00:07:07.860 --> 00:07:10.770
And she goes “You’ve got to be kidding
me!”

00:07:10.770 --> 00:07:15.960
And it’s line in the movie that defines
career commitment.

00:07:15.960 --> 00:07:22.039
She’s not going to go to LA and come back,
she’s going to LA period.

00:07:22.039 --> 00:07:23.970
That’s the end of the story.

00:07:23.970 --> 00:07:30.879
And that’s why movies like that (LA LA LAND)
put artists in tears because they are true

00:07:30.879 --> 00:07:37.919
examples, true stories about what artists
go through when they make decisions to give

00:07:37.919 --> 00:07:39.629
up things.

00:07:39.629 --> 00:07:46.949
I used to give a class at UCLA called ‘Keeping
Your Spirits Up’ for creative people and

00:07:46.949 --> 00:07:52.159
one day I was going around the circle on the
first day of class having people introduce

00:07:52.159 --> 00:07:58.179
themselves (they were mostly actors and actresses
who signed up for it for obvious reasons)

00:07:58.179 --> 00:08:03.819
and the first one I said “Please give us
your name so I can remember your name and

00:08:03.819 --> 00:08:13.729
where you are from and tell us what is the
question you hate to hear most at a party

00:08:13.729 --> 00:08:15.240
in LA?”

00:08:15.240 --> 00:08:21.629
And the first one says “Hi my name is Gayle
and I’m from Detroit and the question I

00:08:21.629 --> 00:08:26.469
hate to hear the most is When are you going
to go back to Detroit and work for the Post

00:08:26.469 --> 00:08:27.469
Office?”

00:08:27.469 --> 00:08:29.389
And I said “How do you react to that?”

00:08:29.389 --> 00:08:32.770
And usually by bursting into tears and running
out of the room.”

00:08:32.770 --> 00:08:34.830
And I say “Oh, okay.

00:08:34.830 --> 00:08:37.710
We’re going to work on that in this class.”

00:08:37.710 --> 00:08:41.740
So I go to the person “My name is Ellis.

00:08:41.740 --> 00:08:48.220
I’m from Southern California and the question
I hate to hear the most at parties is “What

00:08:48.220 --> 00:08:51.570
have you been in big lately that I’ve seen?”

00:08:51.570 --> 00:08:53.020
And I said “And how do you react to that?”

00:08:53.020 --> 00:08:56.500
And she says “I say the Pacific Ocean!”

00:08:56.500 --> 00:09:01.770
So there’s an example of a harnessed person
and an unharnessed person.

00:09:01.770 --> 00:09:07.200
A harnessed person knows that she’s going
to get stupid questions from stupid people

00:09:07.200 --> 00:09:12.310
(insensitive people who aren’t really thinking)
and that there is nothing in the world that’s

00:09:12.310 --> 00:09:17.520
going to stop that from happening so she’s
going to protect herself by coming up with

00:09:17.520 --> 00:09:23.440
an answer beforehand so she doesn’t have
to run out of the room crying.

00:09:23.440 --> 00:09:27.220
And the other person is unharnessed because
she goes out of the room and cries.

00:09:27.220 --> 00:09:32.380
She’s the one that believes that this artistry
thing is some magic, miracle thing that has

00:09:32.380 --> 00:09:36.170
to happen and she doesn’t understand that
it’s mechanical.

00:09:36.170 --> 00:09:40.910
The first thing you have to do is protect
your biggest asset which is your brain.

00:09:40.910 --> 00:09:44.230
And if you haven’t figured out how to do
that then don’t go out in public yet because

00:09:44.230 --> 00:09:48.791
people are not going to start suddenly being
sensitive the moment you appear in public.

00:09:48.791 --> 00:09:49.791
And if you haven’t figured out how to do
that, then don’t go out in public yet because

00:09:49.791 --> 00:09:50.791
people are not suddenly going to start being
sensitive the moment you appear in public.

00:09:50.791 --> 00:09:53.130
They’ve never shown any sign of it in human
history, right?

00:09:53.130 --> 00:09:59.310
I’ve always loved this book I read years
ago as a graduate student called IT BEGAN

00:09:59.310 --> 00:10:03.530
IN BABEL: The history of the world (told with
a sense of humor).

00:10:03.530 --> 00:10:09.090
And the epitaph of the book in the beginning
is “When you really think about it, people

00:10:09.090 --> 00:10:11.720
on the whole are extremely stupid.”

00:10:11.720 --> 00:10:19.430
And it’s a carving on the walls of 1300
BC and it’s kind of comforting to read it

00:10:19.430 --> 00:10:21.650
because you go “Well nothing has changed.

00:10:21.650 --> 00:10:26.680
We’re still there, we’re still stupid
and that’s part of the human comedy.”

00:10:26.680 --> 00:10:31.180
But don’t be stupid if it’s your whole
career at stake.

00:10:31.180 --> 00:10:40.090
Your the one who has to use the tools to build
a defense around you to build a career.

00:10:40.090 --> 00:10:43.590
And people are not going to help you until
they can see that you’ve got that figured

00:10:43.590 --> 00:10:45.180
out and then they’ll help you.

00:10:45.180 --> 00:10:46.810
That’s just the way they are.

00:10:46.810 --> 00:10:50.680
Film Courage: That goes back to what you said
about the sort of crack-pot neighbor in the

00:10:50.680 --> 00:10:55.780
garage making these paintings and everyone
thinks they’re crazy until they sell one

00:10:55.780 --> 00:10:58.920
for a million or two and then all of the sudden
everyone wants to know them.

00:10:58.920 --> 00:11:03.720
Dr. Ken: Yes and then everyone thinks you’re
a genius and predicts…and says “I always

00:11:03.720 --> 00:11:06.120
knew you would do well.”

00:11:06.120 --> 00:11:08.810
Like my father told me I was an idiot when
I became a professor.

00:11:08.810 --> 00:11:13.490
He didn’t understand how I was going to
make a living because I didn’t sign up for

00:11:13.490 --> 00:11:17.160
pre-med (I signed up for classics in college).

00:11:17.160 --> 00:11:24.341
And 19 years later when I told him I was leaving
being a professor for being a producer, he

00:11:24.341 --> 00:11:26.750
told me I was crazy.

00:11:26.750 --> 00:11:28.600
How was I going to make a living?

00:11:28.600 --> 00:11:34.730
And four weeks later he was on the set of
one of my movies and said “This is great.

00:11:34.730 --> 00:11:41.090
Keep doing this.”So if you put a lot of
truck on what other people think, you’ll

00:11:41.090 --> 00:11:42.870
be a mess all your life.

00:11:42.870 --> 00:11:47.400
One of the things I really hate to see is
someone who all about just pleasing people

00:11:47.400 --> 00:11:54.160
and will say whatever they need to say to
please the people in front of them and you

00:11:54.160 --> 00:11:59.590
realize they don’t have a mind of their
own and that’s too bad.

00:11:59.590 --> 00:12:04.780
Too bad to lose your life, to spend your life
without a mind of your own.

00:12:04.780 --> 00:12:11.520
A mind is a terrible thing to loose (as they
say) and the writer I think is somebody who

00:12:11.520 --> 00:12:19.490
has exploited their mind to the max and followed
its lead into places that they would never

00:12:19.490 --> 00:12:28.810
have imagined they would go and I think that’s
a heroic way of life and the world needs more

00:12:28.810 --> 00:12:29.370
storytellers.

